WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.782 --> 00:00:06.490
GREG KUELGEN: Hi.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.840
My name's Greg Kuelgen,
and I'm an architect

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.290
with Apigee at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:13.690
DINO CHIESA: Hi,
I'm Dino Chiesa.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.910
I'm a colleague of Greg's on
the Apigee team at Google Cloud.

00:00:19.065 --> 00:00:20.690
GREG KUELGEN: So I'm
very excited to be

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:23.330
here today to talk to you
about some means of securing

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:23.900
your APIs.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.150
There are a couple of key
points we hope you take away

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.710
from this session.

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:31.550
One-- API security is
not one size fits all.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.130
Depending on the purpose of the
API, the audience, the data,

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.110
et cetera, there'll be
varying techniques you

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:37.898
may use to protect that API.

00:00:37.898 --> 00:00:39.440
While there are
certainly some things

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.960
we'd use base requirements--
for example, transport layer

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.670
security-- the
approaches we talk

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:47.480
about are designed to be layered
and used where appropriate.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.000
Two-- security is not static.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.490
What works today may
need to adapt tomorrow.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:56.220
Three-- tools like
Apigee can help manage

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.380
the security of your APIs.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.440
Dino and I frequently talk to
customers about API security.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.305
Over the past few years, the
number of attacks that we see

00:01:05.305 --> 00:01:07.555
and the number of conversations
we have about security

00:01:07.555 --> 00:01:09.487
have been steadily increasing.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.740
Securing access--
this is generally

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.490
what I believe most
of us think about when

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:18.560
we think API
security, making sure

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.270
that we have authentication
and authorization in place.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.220
As the slide states, the
caller presents something,

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:27.420
and the receiver checks to see
if access should be granted.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.820
In basically all
cases, you want to use

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:35.790
TLS to secure the connection,
also known as one-way TLS.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:38.390
So let's talk about a few
means of actually securing

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:39.350
your access.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:41.320
First, API key.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.830
I really think of
an API key more

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:45.140
as a way to identify a calling
app than a true security

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.930
mechanism, though it's often
a building block for security

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:49.990
mechanisms, such as OAuth.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:52.240
OAuth is a very commonly
used method of authentication

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:53.540
and authorization.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.550
It has allowances to let
a consumer authenticate

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.800
their account and approve
access for certain parts

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:58.882
of their account.

00:01:58.882 --> 00:02:01.090
We've all gone through this
authorization flow, where

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.870
you log into an
account with an app,

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:05.600
it redirects you to another
log-in, such as your Google

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.240
log-in, and asks whether you
grant the app permissions

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.080
to access your data.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.060
In one-way TLS, which is
another means of securing,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.610
the client identifies the
server with the certificate

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.350
presented by that server.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.050
With a two-way TLS connection,
the server similarly

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:23.370
identifies the client.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.600
TLS certificates for clients
can be difficult to manage.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.752
They generally aren't used
with open API programs,

00:02:28.752 --> 00:02:30.960
though they are certainly
used for some partners that

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.510
may access privileged
APIs, often

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.220
in conjunction with other
authentication mechanisms.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.690
For example, third
party payment processors

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.070
may be using a bank's
actual payment APIs--

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.590
often have to have their servers
present client certificates

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.010
as part of their access.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.170
Like I already
mentioned, API key

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:49.970
is really used for
its simplicity.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.590
We highly recommend OAuth.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:53.090
There are different
grant types that

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:54.882
can be utilized based
on the level of trust

00:02:54.882 --> 00:02:56.135
and other requirements.

00:02:56.135 --> 00:02:58.160
A simple client
credentials grant type,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.350
where a system passes a
client ID and a secret,

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:03.320
might work well for a
trusted internal system.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:05.840
A third party app that allows
access to individual data,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.780
on other hand, would need to
use an authorization code flow,

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.210
like I previously described,
where the consumer authorizes

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:12.810
the access.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.365
Finally, I'll mention that
TLS certs used for two-way TLS

00:03:16.365 --> 00:03:18.740
make a lot of sense when you're
using a service mesh that

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.912
can manage all of that for you.

00:03:20.912 --> 00:03:23.120
One of the things in common
with all of these methods

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.780
is that they identify
the calling client.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:28.580
OAuth tokens, however, can also
identify not just the client,

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:30.890
but the end user as well.

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:32.580
So Dino, what
situations do you see

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.330
customers using these
different mechanisms

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:37.150
to secure their API access?

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.680
DINO CHIESA: Well, the API key
is a good basic building block.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.870
It's appropriate for use with
an open API, where you want

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.180
to track who's calling the API.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.210
And also, it can be
used as a key identifier

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.910
when doing signing
or encryption.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.540
I'll talk more about that later.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.610
OAuth is pretty
common, as you said.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.710
Most API gateways
will just support it.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.330
It's a really good idea.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.360
Two-way TLS, that's going
to work for devices that are

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:04.990
managed--

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:08.500
point of sale terminals,
devices that you control.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.310
Not so easy for consumer
mobile devices or even partner

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:14.547
systems.

00:04:14.547 --> 00:04:15.380
GREG KUELGEN: Great.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.010
What about JSON web
tokens, or "jots"?

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.510
Can I use those for
authorization access?

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:21.620
DINO CHIESA: Sure you can.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.920
JWT is just a special
kind of token.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.720
OAuth bearer tokens are commonly
opaque, a random string.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.480
No information is inherent
within that string.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.130
A JWT is different.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.550
It encodes actual claims.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.580
With an opaque token, only
the authorization server

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.520
that issued the token
can resolve that token

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.260
into a set of claims
or entitlements.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:49.580
In contrast, any system
can decode and verify

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:52.370
the information in a signed JWT.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.535
So yes, JWT can be
used as a bearer token

00:04:55.535 --> 00:04:57.890
to support authorization
checks, and it

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:02.750
may be more flexible and
preferred in some cases.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.120
When to use each?

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.370
As always, it depends.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:11.040
That's a larger conversation.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.630
GREG KUELGEN: Spoken like a
true architect-- it depends.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.710
I've used that phrase
many times myself.

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:20.280
Now let's talk about securing
your API messages themselves.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.800
I've worked with a customer
that was dealing with consumer

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:24.078
credit information.

00:05:24.078 --> 00:05:25.620
As you might imagine,
they're passing

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:27.960
around personally
identifiable information

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.570
that we would all
prefer not be leaked.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.120
They use things like TLS
and OAuth, of course,

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.620
but in addition,
they actually chose

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.460
to pass some incoming payloads
as a JWT using JSON web

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:39.390
encryption.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:40.890
For those transactions,
it gave them

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.120
an extra layer of security.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.035
Even if someone got
a valid OAuth token,

00:05:44.035 --> 00:05:46.410
they still needed to sign and
encrypt the message itself.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.630
At a high level,
there are really

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.970
two key items when it
comes to message security--

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:56.250
signatures and encryption.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:01.200
Signatures provide message
integrity and non-repudiation.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.840
Basically, a signature can
contain a hash of the message

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.310
and is often signed
using a secret

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.230
only the signer
possesses, but can

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.570
be verified by the receiver.

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.600
In this way, we can verify that
the message hasn't been altered

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.012
and has actually been sent
by the intended sender.

00:06:15.012 --> 00:06:17.220
Signatures don't provide
any privacy for the message,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:17.870
though.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.490
For that, you need encryption.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.300
A combination of
signatures and encryption

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.360
can be used to protect
sensitive data.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.290
HMAC is also used pretty
widely by cloud providers.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:28.960
For example, in
GCP, you can create

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.840
a side URL that allows
distributed access to a storage

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.340
bucket.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.900
Using encryption
can be useful if you

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.660
want to protect against things
like a TLS vulnerability

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:40.150
that would allow an attacker
to view data in flight.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:41.620
It can also be
useful to limit who

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.810
is able to see data as
it moves between systems

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:45.907
or when stored on
disk along the way.

00:06:45.907 --> 00:06:47.490
Generally speaking,
this is only going

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.473
to be used for your
most sensitive data.

00:06:49.473 --> 00:06:50.890
This leads me to
another question.

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.050
Dino, do you have any
recommendations for when

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.560
folks should use these different
message security mechanisms?

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.310
DINO CHIESA: Sure.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:58.480
Here are a few.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.730
First, keep it simple.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:04.360
Signing or encryption
necessarily means more work

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.150
for client developers
and more complexity.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:09.970
Take care to apply
it thoughtfully.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:13.240
Second, application layer
signing or encryption

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.510
helps protecting information
across multiple hops

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.490
in a chain.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:22.850
Third, select the signing
approach that fits your team.

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:27.250
So HMAC, RSA-- whichever
they're most comfortable with.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:30.280
And don't forget, JWT can
be used to sign or encrypt

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:33.280
payloads, not just ID tokens.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:35.110
In all these cases,
you're going to need

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.810
to think about key management.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:41.260
Last, include a timestamp
in the signed payload

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.530
to protect against replays.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:45.200
JWT will help you with this.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:48.250
If you use HMAC or RSA,
you will have to come up

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:49.986
with your own convention.

00:07:52.847 --> 00:07:53.680
GREG KUELGEN: Great.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.420
Thanks, Dino.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:58.300
Securing conversations-- even
with other security measures

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.890
in place, sometimes
attackers create

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.795
accounts to try to mimic
human users and gain access.

00:08:02.795 --> 00:08:05.170
Rarely, though, do these
attacks follow the same patterns

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.520
as normal use.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:09.370
Securing conversations generally
makes use of modern technology

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:11.890
like machine learning to
consider usage patterns

00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:14.523
and potentially identify
malicious traffic.

00:08:14.523 --> 00:08:16.690
This is where we start to
get into things like bots.

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:18.900
There are plenty of
bots with bad intent.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.677
It can take advanced features
to help identify and prevent

00:08:21.677 --> 00:08:22.510
this type of attack.

00:08:24.968 --> 00:08:26.510
There are a few
things in the toolkit

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.050
for securing conversations.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.090
One of the simplest
can be rate limiting.

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:31.910
It's unlikely, for
example, that a human

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.820
will be requesting a new
token several times a second,

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:37.309
or even more than once a minute.

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:39.679
Anomaly detection has also
seen an uptick with customers

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:41.030
that I support.

00:08:41.030 --> 00:08:43.298
As we talked about, sometimes
bad actors get through

00:08:43.298 --> 00:08:45.590
and that can change the error
rate, or the overall rate

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:47.028
of traffic, even.

00:08:47.028 --> 00:08:49.070
Anomaly detection applies
machine learning models

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.450
to understand what
a normal traffic

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:53.033
flow looks like, and can alert
when things like the error rate

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:54.680
are outside of normal.

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:56.960
This is all done without
anyone actually configuring

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:58.550
a specific alert.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.740
Security teams have come
to rely on these alerts

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.470
as a potential indicator
that a bad actor is bypassing

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:05.608
other security mechanisms.

00:09:05.608 --> 00:09:08.150
It also works great to alert to
potential operational issues,

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:09.890
and I've had more
than one customer that

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.080
has told me anomaly detection
has helped them detect

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.270
an error, alert the
target service that

00:09:14.270 --> 00:09:16.400
was having the
issue, and fix it all

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.220
before they broke their SLO.

00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:21.295
You'll also see
reCAPTCHA up here.

00:09:21.295 --> 00:09:22.670
Usually when I
think of reCAPTCHA

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:24.420
I think of having to
click on the annoying

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.140
images or maybe type some
difficult-to-read characters.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:31.520
Hey Dino, how could reCAPTCHA
help with securing APIs?

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:32.670
DINO CHIESA: Good question.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:35.120
So in the original
version of reCAPTCHA,

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:40.460
a web page presents the familiar
"are you a robot" challenge.

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:43.610
Google released version
3 of reCAPTCHA in 2018.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:47.300
In that version, it now
relies on device and endpoint

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:48.130
fingerprinting.

00:09:48.130 --> 00:09:49.880
There is no UI.

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:53.690
That means both web
pages and APIs can be

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:55.320
protected by the same system.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.770
The client needs to connect
to the reCAPTCHA server

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:58.960
to get a token.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:03.820
That client uses that token in
its request for an API token.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:07.300
The API server can then
validate the reCAPTCHA token

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:09.790
before granting service,
and reCAPTCHA will issue

00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:11.350
an opinion about that client--

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:14.230
is it a robot or is it not?

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:16.450
Using reCAPTCHA
this way, reCAPTCHA

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:19.900
acts as a gatekeeper or
bouncer for your front door.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:23.440
It does mean introducing
a new external dependency

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:25.090
into your system,
but you may want

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:29.860
to accept that to get the
additional level of security.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:30.790
GREG KUELGEN: Perfect.

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:33.460
Thank you.

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.100
So when do you use what?

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:39.820
API access token pretty much
is always going to be in place.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:41.860
There are some exceptions
for truly public info,

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:43.443
but even then, you
may want to provide

00:10:43.443 --> 00:10:45.430
some level of access control.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:46.960
Signatures can be
useful when you

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.240
need to verify the
integrity of the payload,

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:51.340
or can even be used
for distributed access,

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:53.482
like the signed URL example.

00:10:53.482 --> 00:10:54.940
Encryption is useful
when a message

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:56.590
may be passed around
between multiple systems

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.050
in an asynchronous manner, or
for the most sensitive data

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.425
that you want to
ensure that even

00:11:00.425 --> 00:11:03.437
if the wires are compromised,
the content is still secure.

00:11:03.437 --> 00:11:05.770
This can also be used to
ensure that messages are secure

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.170
even when written to disk
or if they're inadvertently

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.090
written to logs.

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:12.490
There are multiple areas
where anomaly to bot detection

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:13.958
might make sense,
but we recommend

00:11:13.958 --> 00:11:16.000
that this is at least used
to protect your log-in

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.178
and token API.

00:11:17.178 --> 00:11:19.720
Ideally, you want to prevent
the bad actors from ever getting

00:11:19.720 --> 00:11:21.490
a token in the first place.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:23.200
With that, let me
turn it over to Dino,

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.617
who will walk us through how
we might be able to implement

00:11:25.617 --> 00:11:28.007
some of this inside of Apigee.

00:11:28.007 --> 00:11:30.340
DINO CHIESA: Information-driven
companies will have tens

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.590
or hundreds of services
that they use to run

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.770
their business-- some of them
custom-built, maybe in Java,

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:39.520
Golang, C#, Python,
maybe running in a VM

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:40.270
or in Kubernetes.

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:43.360
Some of these services
might be third party--

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.530
SaaS.

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.440
There are many different
implementation options.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.410
Apigee can help
manage all of them.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.860
To do that, we rely
on an Apigee proxy

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:58.670
that intercedes between clients
of the service and the service.

00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:02.170
This allows us to standardize
on the access, message,

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:05.930
and conversation security.

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:09.290
For this demonstration, I have
a single upstream service.

00:12:09.290 --> 00:12:12.440
It's implemented in Node.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.180
It uses Express.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:15.800
It's JavaScript.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:18.420
And all those
details don't matter.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:25.910
The key detail is that
it exposes an HTTP API.

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:28.640
So let me show you what it
looks like to invoke that.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.410
The service is running locally.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:32.070
I have my dev instance.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:34.160
And you can see it's an
inventory and product

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:35.240
management service.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.700
I can query it and get
information about products.

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:42.450
You can see Anvil, Birdseed,
a bunch of other products.

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:43.850
This is just the dev instance.

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:47.330
I also have it running
in Google App Engine.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.000
So lets try to invoke it there.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.660
There's a different URL.

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:52.380
I'll send the same request.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:55.340
And you can see I'm getting
a 401 unauthorized--

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:57.920
the service is locked down.

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.020
To enforce the
API management, we

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.300
must ensure that the
service is accessible only

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:04.250
through the API gateway.

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:08.570
We can do that with two-way TLS,
firewall configuration with IP

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:13.520
restrictions, or via
some other mechanism.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:16.940
Apigee then acts as the
enforcement point for clients.

00:13:16.940 --> 00:13:20.450
No client can reach the service
except through the Apigee

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:20.960
proxy.

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.510
So let me show you a
proxy that stands in front

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.910
of this particular service.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.230
So here we see the
target of the proxy,

00:13:30.230 --> 00:13:34.970
and this is the ingress
endpoint for the API proxy.

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:38.900
The proxy itself is
just a pass-through.

00:13:38.900 --> 00:13:41.760
It receives the request and
then proxies to the upstream.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:44.390
So let me turn on tracing,
and then we'll flip back

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.780
and we'll invoke not
the Google App Engine

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:50.510
endpoint, but the proxy
endpoint in Apigee.

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:52.400
You can see I'm sending
that same request,

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:55.340
and it's actually connecting
to that App Engine

00:13:55.340 --> 00:13:57.920
instance, which,
because it's Apigee,

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.120
it's allowing it through.

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.700
And you can see I've got
a trace statement here.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.350
The request is
received, and then it's

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:06.830
proxied to the upstream target.

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:10.520
So really nice, except
Apigee is merely

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:12.870
acting as a pass-through.

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:15.540
We want the proxy to do more.

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:19.760
So in order to illustrate
that, let's stop the trace.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:26.330
We'll deploy version 12 of
this proxy, which includes

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:29.805
a verify access token policy.

00:14:29.805 --> 00:14:31.180
So now we're going
to start doing

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:33.830
OAuth enforcement in the proxy.

00:14:33.830 --> 00:14:36.020
Again, let me turn on
a trace, and then I'm

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:38.960
going to run that same request.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.930
And you should see Apigee
saying, hey, unauthorized.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:44.730
You did not pass a token.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.330
So not going to let you in.

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:48.860
How can we get a token?

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:52.400
Apigee also acts as
a token dispensary,

00:14:52.400 --> 00:14:53.840
and I have another
proxy for that.

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:57.590
This is an Apigee
OAuth proxy that

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:02.220
generates tokens for their
client credentials grant type.

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:05.210
So I'm going to turn
on trace for that,

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:12.180
and now we'll request a token
using client credentials.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.400
So that's a different
API request.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.520
And you can see I'm
sending that into Apigee.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:20.160
And yep, I've got an
access token coming back.

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:21.080
So that's really good.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.980
In the trace, you can see
Apigee handled that request,

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:25.970
it verified the
client credentials,

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:27.560
and then generated
a token, which

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:32.870
is now good for 30 minutes.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.100
We now have the token.

00:15:34.100 --> 00:15:37.180
Let's invoke the service again.

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:39.080
We'll switch back
to the other proxy.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:43.160
We'll invoke the service again,
passing that valid token.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.455
And now you can see Apigee
is receiving the request.

00:15:45.455 --> 00:15:49.580
It includes the valid token,
and I'm getting the product

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:51.210
inventory information.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:52.790
So that's just what we want.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.850
What if I use an
expired access token?

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:58.280
Something that's out of date?

00:15:58.280 --> 00:15:59.600
Also want to check that out.

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:01.820
Want to make sure
that access tokens get

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:03.740
rejected when they are expired.

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:06.270
And sure enough, Apigee
is sending back, hey,

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:07.310
that token is old.

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:08.750
That's expired.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:12.120
It's doing exactly
what we want it to do.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:15.750
Let's try an update.

00:16:15.750 --> 00:16:18.740
For this part of
the demonstration,

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:19.880
I have a payload.

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:21.260
I want to send a post in.

00:16:21.260 --> 00:16:24.710
The payload looks like that,
and I've configured the Apigee

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:30.440
proxy in here to check
the HMAC on that payload

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:34.550
to prevent replays and to ensure
greater message security--

00:16:34.550 --> 00:16:37.070
something we talked about
a little bit earlier.

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:42.410
So let's try to send
that in, send in a post

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:47.040
with that update, with
that payload as an update.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.470
And you can see
Apigee is sending,

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:53.510
hey, you need to send an
HMAC for that request, which

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:55.340
is, again, what we want.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:16:59.840
Let's try sending in that
request with an invalid HMAC--

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:02.570
the wrong HMAC for this payload.

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.280
You can see now
Apigee is saying,

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:08.280
ah, well, you sent in an HMAC,
but it was the wrong HMAC.

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:10.359
That HMAC did not succeed.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:13.843
And here's the one where
we didn't include an HMAC.

00:17:13.843 --> 00:17:15.510
Here's Apigee handling
the request where

00:17:15.510 --> 00:17:17.920
we did send an HMAC, but
it was the wrong one.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.400
This is the actual
correct HMAC value

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:23.579
that Apigee is expecting
for this given payload.

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:24.930
This is the value that I sent.

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:29.010
So let's send the correct value.

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:33.270
HMAC equals that value, and
we'll send that same request

00:17:33.270 --> 00:17:36.120
again with the correct value.

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:38.640
And now Apigee said,
yep, that's good.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:43.320
That post, we've validated
it, Apigee has validated it.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:47.670
The HMAC passed, and then
it proxies to the upstream.

00:17:47.670 --> 00:17:50.100
The upstream system did
not have to concern itself

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.970
with tokens or HMACs.

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:57.400
Apigee is doing all
that work for you.

00:17:57.400 --> 00:17:59.720
So I didn't show it
here, but remember,

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:03.000
you'll want to include
timestamps in your HMACs

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:04.515
to prevent replays.

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:10.090
In the prior
examples, the client

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:12.280
used a client
credentials grant type,

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.700
passing in the client ID in
secret in the basic auth header

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.360
in order to get a token.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:20.260
How about using a JWT
as an alternative?

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:22.750
Instead of passing a
client ID in secret,

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:25.060
the token endpoint
in Apigee can require

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:30.130
that the client signs
the request for token

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:32.480
using a key payer provision
just for this client.

00:18:32.480 --> 00:18:36.700
And we can use as the
signing mechanism JWT--

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:37.870
that's standard.

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.220
So how do we do that?

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:41.755
First, we'll get a JWT.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:49.240
And I'll use this
web page to generate

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:51.910
a signed JWT using
the private key that

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:53.560
is associated to this client.

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:56.240
Let me grab that JWT.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:58.970
And we'll set that
as a shell variable.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:05.500
Now I want to open up
the JWT token dispensary.

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:09.150
I'm going to turn
on tracing there.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.800
And then we're going to send
in a request for a token that

00:19:11.800 --> 00:19:14.581
passes that JWT.

00:19:14.581 --> 00:19:18.100
So here's a new request for
token, passing in the JWT

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.370
that I just set.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.660
Here's the grant type-- it's
a JWT bearer grant type.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:27.310
And now I've got an opaque
access token in exchange

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:31.620
for that signed assertion
from the client.

00:19:31.620 --> 00:19:33.970
And that opaque
token will work just

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:38.300
like a token issued with
client credentials grant

00:19:38.300 --> 00:19:43.220
type, so that's going to
work just as expected.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:50.000
What if we use a JWT
ID token to get--

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:52.950
as an exchange for
a bearer token?

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:54.860
So here, this is
a page that allows

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:58.940
me to sign in using Google
Sign-In, which is just

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:01.730
an OpenID Connect provider.

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:05.870
And what that means is
it will issue an ID token

00:20:05.870 --> 00:20:10.430
to an app that allows
a person to sign in.

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:14.185
So now I've got a JWT from
Google that's much bigger.

00:20:14.185 --> 00:20:15.560
It's got a lot
more claims in it.

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.050
You can see some of the
claims here are decoded--

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:22.820
the email address, the name, a
URL for my picture, and so on.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.370
What if we use that
to try to get a token?

00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:31.730
We can do that here with a
new, different API proxy.

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:36.830
So this one, rather than
verifying a self-signed JWT

00:20:36.830 --> 00:20:39.530
from a client, it's
going to verify

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:43.760
that this is a token
issued by Google Sign-In.

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:47.210
You can use any OpenID
Connect provider--

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:51.080
oxAuth, Auth0, Azure,
Google Sign-In.

00:20:51.080 --> 00:20:54.050
There are lots of other
options, but any of them

00:20:54.050 --> 00:20:58.890
will have that same kind of
approach with a JWKS endpoint.

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:06.970
So let me try now obtaining
a token with that JWT.

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:09.050
We use a different endpoint.

00:21:09.050 --> 00:21:12.830
And sure enough, I'm getting
now an access token in exchange

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:15.420
for an ID token.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:17.390
So that's a really nice
capability in Apigee.

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:21.260
And you can see the flow
in Apigee allowing that--

00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:23.920
verifying that request and
issuing the token back.

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:25.820
And again, that
token will work just

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:29.600
like any other token
issued by Apigee.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:31.590
How about something
a little different?

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:35.820
What if we want Apigee
to accept a JWT directly?

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:39.380
So I've got a different API
proxy endpoint for that.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:44.540
This one accepts a JWT, and
it just verifies that JWT.

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:48.420
Again, this will be an ID
token issued by Google.

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:56.080
So we'll turn on tracing for
that API proxy and invoke that,

00:21:56.080 --> 00:22:00.650
and you can see the API
proxy is now accepting

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:04.250
the JWT as an input token.

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:05.850
And it's verifying
that directly.

00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:06.950
So no opaque token.

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:12.440
Rather, it's the full ID token
issued by Google Sign-In.

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:18.260
And as I said, you can use
any OpenID Connect provider

00:22:18.260 --> 00:22:20.720
to allow that to happen.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:22.280
Last thing I want
to show is what

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:25.040
about if we want to protect
the token dispensary

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:27.350
endpoint with reCAPTCHA?

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:31.290
And I've got another API
proxy here that does that.

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:35.840
So again, this is a client
credentials token dispensary

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:41.060
in Apigee, but it protects
the request with reCAPTCHA.

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:43.700
And the way this works
will become clearer

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:45.530
after I show you.

00:22:45.530 --> 00:22:50.795
The client page looks like this.

00:22:53.750 --> 00:22:57.250
So this is a bit of JavaScript
that runs in a browser,

00:22:57.250 --> 00:23:03.420
and it uses the reCAPTCHA
SDK from Google.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:08.130
Here, I use the client
to obtain a token,

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:12.220
a reCAPTCHA token using
the reCAPTCHA site key.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:16.240
Then after the client
gets the reCAPTCHA token,

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:20.350
it can send in a
request for an API token

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:23.320
to Apigee, passing in
the reCAPTCHA token

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.180
as part of that request.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:31.240
Then Apigee, in the proxy, will
verify that reCAPTCHA token--

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:37.500
it does that here-- before
issuing a client credentials

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:38.930
grant-type token.

00:23:38.930 --> 00:23:40.450
So let's see this all work.

00:23:40.450 --> 00:23:43.360
I've got the page running
that, and I'll just

00:23:43.360 --> 00:23:44.500
click this button.

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:48.730
It's obtaining the
token from reCAPTCHA,

00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:54.040
sending that along with a client
credentials grant into Apigee,

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:59.320
and you can see that Apigee
received that request

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:02.650
and verified the token.

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:06.520
Google replied with a
response for that token,

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:12.700
giving it a score of
0.9, which is sufficient.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:16.810
So the Apigee proxy
issues the token

00:24:16.810 --> 00:24:18.865
as it did in all
those other cases.

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:23.400
And that's it.

00:24:23.400 --> 00:24:26.240
I hope that gives you a good
idea of some of the things

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:29.660
that you can do with Apigee
for access security, message

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:33.350
security, and
conversation security.

00:24:33.350 --> 00:24:36.030
OK, those
demonstrations, I hope,

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:38.850
give you an idea of some
of the possibilities

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:42.600
Apigee offers for
securing your APIs,

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:46.020
focusing on securing access,
securing the messages

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:50.860
themselves, and monitoring and
securing patterns of messages.

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:54.213
So now, I'll turn it back to
Greg to bring it home for us.

00:24:54.213 --> 00:24:55.130
GREG KUELGEN: Awesome.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:55.672
Thanks, Dino.

00:24:55.672 --> 00:24:56.678
Great demo.

00:24:56.678 --> 00:24:58.220
Today we learned
about different ways

00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:01.880
that you can secure access,
most commonly using tokens.

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:04.682
You can secure your API messages
with signatures and encryption,

00:25:04.682 --> 00:25:06.140
and finally, how
advanced features,

00:25:06.140 --> 00:25:08.000
such as anomaly
detection and reCAPTCHA,

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:10.273
can help secure
your conversations.

00:25:10.273 --> 00:25:11.690
You saw these
different techniques

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:13.970
can be applied differently
to different APIs,

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:15.560
briefly see how
you could actually

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:16.670
implement some of these--

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:20.300
this functionality quickly
and easy using Apigee.

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:22.520
I want to truly thank you
for watching this session,

